Snowflake Secure Data Sharing — Complete Guide
Detailed Summary
What is Secure Data Sharing?
Secure Data Sharing is the ability for one Snowflake account to grant read-only access to selected database objects (tables, views, etc.) to another Snowflake account — without transferring any data between accounts.

Key Roles
Role	Description
Data Provider	The account that owns and shares the data (e.g. Account A)
Data Consumer	The account that receives and reads the shared data (e.g. Account B)
How It Works Under the Hood
Enabled by Snowflake's Global Services Layer
The provider exposes their own storage via the services layer to another account's queries
No data is physically moved or copied — the consumer reads directly from the provider's storage
Consumer pays only for compute costs to query the shared data — no storage costs
What Can Be Shared?
Object	Shareable?	Notes
Tables	YES	
External Tables	YES	
Secure Views	YES	Recommended over sharing tables directly
Secure Materialized Views	YES	
Secure UDFs	YES	
Regular Views	NO	Must be designated as SECURE first
Regular UDFs	NO	Must be designated as SECURE first
Snowflake strongly recommends sharing Secure Views over Tables — they hide the view definition and protect against inadvertently exposing source table data, which is critical when sharing with external organizations.

The Share Object
A Share is an account-level object created by the data provider. It has two configurations:

Grants — which database objects to share (what to share)
Accounts — which consumer accounts can access the share (who to share with)
Provider Setup — Step by Step
Step 1 — Create the Share object

Step 2 — Grant access to database objects in this order:

Grant access to the Database (only ONE database per share)
Grant access to one or more Schemas
Grant access to specific Database Objects (tables, views, etc.)
Step 3 — Add consumer accounts to the share (virtually unlimited accounts)

Consumer Setup — Step by Step
Step 1 — List available shares using SHOW SHARES

Step 2 — Create a database from the share (only ONE database per share)

Step 3 — Query the shared objects as if they were native tables in their own account, including joining with their own data

Important Provider Behaviors
Behavior	Detail
Objects added to share	Immediately available to ALL consumers
New objects created in shared DB	NOT automatically added to share — must be explicitly granted
Future grants	Cannot be used in shares
One database per share	Hard limit — only one database per share
Number of shares	No hard limit on shares a provider can create
Number of consumer accounts	No hard limit on accounts added to a share
Role required	ACCOUNTADMIN by default (can be delegated via CREATE SHARE privilege)
Revoking access	Access can be revoked at ANY time — instantly removes consumer access
Important Consumer Behaviors
Behavior	Detail
Databases per share	Only ONE database can be created per share
Time Travel	NOT available on shared tables
Cloning	Cannot clone shared tables
Creating objects	Cannot create objects inside the shared database
Resharing	Cannot reshare shared database objects
Role required	ACCOUNTADMIN by default (can be delegated via IMPORT SHARE privilege)
Cross-Region Sharing Limitation
Secure Data Sharing only works between accounts in the same region and cloud platform
To share across regions, the provider must:
Replicate the database into an account within the same region as the consumer
Create a share from the replicated database
Reader Accounts (Managed Accounts)
For scenarios where the consumer does not have a Snowflake account:

Aspect	Detail
What it is	A limited Snowflake account provisioned by the provider for non-Snowflake users
Purpose	Query and view shared database objects only
Access	URL, username, and password provided
Limitations	Cannot insert data, cannot copy data in, can only read from the provider's share
Share restriction	Can only access shares from the provider account that created it
Billing	All compute costs billed back to the provider account
Limit	Soft limit of 20 reader accounts per provider (can be increased via Snowflake Support)
Agreement	Consumer does NOT need to enter a Snowflake agreement
Snowflake Data Marketplace
A portal within the Snowflake UI that enables data monetization
Providers can set up pay-for-access models for their shared data
Built on top of the Secure Data Sharing infrastructure
Snowflake Editions Supporting Secure Data Sharing
Edition	Supported?
Standard	YES
Enterprise	YES
Business Critical	YES
Virtual Private Snowflake (VPS)	NO
Interview Questions & Answers
Conceptual Questions
Q1. What is Secure Data Sharing in Snowflake and how does it work?

Secure Data Sharing allows one Snowflake account (the data provider) to grant read-only access to selected database objects to another Snowflake account (the data consumer) without physically transferring any data. It works through Snowflake's Global Services Layer, which exposes the provider's storage to the consumer's queries. The consumer reads data directly from the provider's storage — no data movement occurs. As a result, the consumer only pays for compute resources to run queries, not for any storage.

Q2. What is the difference between a Data Provider and a Data Consumer?

Data Provider — the account that owns the data and creates the Share object. They control which database objects are shared and which accounts can access them. They can revoke access at any time.
Data Consumer — the account that receives access to the shared data. They create a database from the share and can query the shared objects as if they were native to their own account. However, they cannot insert, update, clone, or reshare the shared data.
Q3. Why does Snowflake recommend sharing Secure Views over Tables?

When sharing directly with external organizations, exposing raw tables can risk revealing more data than intended. Secure Views:

Hide the view definition — the consumer cannot see how the view was built or what the underlying table structure looks like
Protect against inadvertent data exposure — the view can filter or mask sensitive columns before sharing
Add an extra security layer — since you are potentially sharing with a completely different organization, stricter data protection is advisable
Regular views cannot be shared — they must be explicitly designated as SECURE first.

Q4. What is a Share object and what does it consist of?

A Share is an account-level object created by the data provider. When first created it is an empty container with no configuration. It consists of two configurations:

Grants — permissions applied to the share defining which database objects consumers can read (decides WHAT to share)
Accounts — the list of consumer accounts that can see and access the share (decides WHO to share with)
Only the ACCOUNTADMIN role can create shares by default, though this can be delegated via the CREATE SHARE privilege.

Technical Questions
Q5. What database objects can be included in a Snowflake Share?

The following objects can be shared:

Tables
External Tables
Secure Views (must be designated SECURE)
Secure Materialized Views (must be designated SECURE)
Secure UDFs (must be designated SECURE)
Regular views and UDFs cannot be shared unless they are first designated as SECURE. This designation hides the definition and adds an extra layer of data protection.

Q6. Walk through the complete steps to set up data sharing from both provider and consumer sides.

Provider Side:

Create a Share object (requires ACCOUNTADMIN or CREATE SHARE privilege)
Grant USAGE on the database to the share
Grant USAGE on the relevant schema(s) to the share
Grant SELECT on the specific tables/views to the share
Add the consumer account(s) to the share
Consumer Side:

Use SHOW SHARES to list available shares
Use DESCRIBE SHARE to see what objects are included
Create a database from the share (requires ACCOUNTADMIN or IMPORT SHARE privilege)
Query the shared objects as normal
Q7. What are the key limitations of Secure Data Sharing?

Provider limitations:

Only ONE database can be added per share
Future grants cannot be used in shares — new objects must be explicitly granted
Cross-region sharing is not natively supported
Consumer limitations:

Only ONE database can be created from a share
Time Travel is NOT available on shared tables
Cannot clone shared tables
Cannot create objects inside the shared database
Cannot reshare shared objects with other accounts
Platform limitation:

Not supported on Virtual Private Snowflake (VPS)
Cross-region sharing requires database replication first
Q8. What happens when a data provider revokes access to a shared object?

Access is instantly removed. If a provider:

Removes a grant from a share — the consumer immediately loses access to that object
Drops a shared object or data — the consumer's access to that object is instantly revoked
There is no delay or grace period. This gives providers complete, real-time control over what consumers can see.

Q9. A new table is created inside a shared database. Is it automatically visible to consumers?

No. Any new object created within a shared database is NOT automatically added to the share. The provider must explicitly add a new grant to the share for that object to make it accessible to consumers. Future grants cannot be used in shares — every object must be individually and explicitly granted.

Q10. What is a Reader Account and when would you use it?

A Reader Account (also called a Managed Account) is a limited Snowflake account provisioned by a data provider for organizations that do not have their own Snowflake account. It is used when:

A provider wants to share data with a non-Snowflake customer or partner
The consumer does not want to enter a Snowflake agreement
Key characteristics:

Consumer is given a URL, username, and password
Can only query shared objects from the provider account that created it
Cannot insert data or create their own objects
All compute costs are billed back to the provider
Soft limit of 20 reader accounts per provider (can be raised via Snowflake Support)
Q11. How would you share data with a consumer in a different cloud region?

Direct cross-region sharing is not natively supported in Snowflake. The workaround involves two steps:

Replicate the database you want to share into a Snowflake account that is in the same region and cloud platform as the consumer
Create a Share from that replicated database and add the consumer account
This combines two features — Replication (for cross-region data movement) and Secure Data Sharing (for the actual sharing mechanism).

Q12. Who pays for the compute costs when a consumer queries shared data?

It depends on the type of consumer:

Standard consumer account — the consumer pays for compute resources used to query shared data. They do not pay any storage costs since no data is copied to their account.
Reader account — the provider pays for ALL compute costs incurred by the reader account, since the provider created and is responsible for that account.
Scenario-Based Questions
Q13. A financial data company wants to sell access to their market data to clients. Some clients have Snowflake, some don't. How would you set this up?

For clients with Snowflake accounts:

Create a Share with grants on the relevant secure views
Add client Snowflake accounts to the share
Use the Data Marketplace to manage and monetize access
For clients without Snowflake accounts:

Provision a Reader Account for each client (up to 20, soft limit)
Provide them with the account URL, username, and password
All compute costs for reader account queries are billed back to the provider
Contact Snowflake Support if more than 20 reader accounts are needed
Q14. A consumer says they want to modify the shared data and save it in their account. Is this possible?

Not directly. Consumers have read-only access to shared data — they cannot insert, update, or delete shared data, nor can they clone shared tables or create objects inside the shared database.

However, they can achieve a similar outcome by:

Running a CREATE TABLE AS SELECT to copy the shared data into a native table in their own database (outside the shared database)
They would then own and have full control over that copied table
This does incur storage costs in the consumer account for the copied data
Q15. What is the role of the Global Services Layer in Secure Data Sharing?

The Global Services Layer is what makes zero-copy data sharing possible. Rather than physically transferring data, the provider exposes their storage layer to the consumer through the Global Services Layer. When the consumer runs a query against shared objects, the query is routed through the services layer to read directly from the provider's underlying storage. This means:

No data duplication occurs
Consumers see the most up-to-date data instantly
No storage costs for the consumer
Changes made by the provider (including revoking access) take effect immediately
